=========================================================
OBJECT SLICING (INTERVIEW NOTES) ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐
=========================================================

DEFINITION
----------

Object Slicing occurs when a Derived object is copied
into a Base object by value.

Only the Base-class portion is copied.

The Derived-specific data and behavior are lost.

=========================================================
EXAMPLE
=========================================================

#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Employee
{
public:
    string name;

    Employee(string n) : name(n) {}

    virtual void display()
    {
        cout << "Employee: " << name << endl;
    }

    virtual ~Employee() = default;
};

class Manager : public Employee
{
public:
    int teamSize;

    Manager(string n, int size)
        : Employee(n), teamSize(size) {}

    void display() override
    {
        cout << "Manager: " << name
             << ", Team Size: " << teamSize << endl;
    }
};

// Passed by VALUE -> Object Slicing
void show(Employee emp)
{
    emp.display();
}

int main()
{
    Manager m("Alice", 10);

    show(m);

    return 0;
}

Output:
Employee: Alice

=========================================================
WHY DOES THIS HAPPEN?
=========================================================

Original Object

Manager
------------------------------
name = Alice
teamSize = 10
------------------------------

          Copy by Value
                ↓

Employee
------------------------------
name = Alice
------------------------------

Only the Employee part is copied.

The Manager-specific data (teamSize) is discarded.

Since emp is now an actual Employee object,

emp.display()

calls

Employee::display()

instead of

Manager::display()

=========================================================
HOW TO AVOID OBJECT SLICING?
=========================================================

Pass by reference:

void show(Employee& emp)
{
    emp.display();
}

No copy is created.

Employee& refers to the original Manager object.

Output:
Manager: Alice, Team Size: 10

=========================================================
WHEN DOES OBJECT SLICING OCCUR?
=========================================================

✓ Base b = derived;

✓ Function(Base obj);

✓ Returning Base by value;

✓ Storing Derived objects in vector<Base>

=========================================================
HOW TO AVOID OBJECT SLICING?
=========================================================

Use:

✓ Base*

✓ Base&

Instead of:

✗ Base object

"In this example, Manager inherits from Employee and overrides the
display() function.

The function show() takes an Employee object by value.

When I pass a Manager object to show(), the compiler creates a new
Employee object by copying only the Employee portion of Manager.
The Manager-specific part, such as teamSize, is discarded. This is
called object slicing.

Since the copied object is now an actual Employee object, the dynamic
type becomes Employee. Therefore, even though display() is virtual,
Employee::display() is invoked instead of Manager::display().

The original Manager object is unaffected, so calling m.display()
afterwards correctly invokes Manager::display().

To avoid object slicing, I would pass the object by reference or
pointer instead of by value."

=========================================================
MOST ASKED INTERVIEW QUESTION
=========================================================

Q. Why is Object Slicing dangerous?

Answer:

Object slicing copies only the base-class portion of a
derived object, discarding all derived-specific data.
The copied object becomes a pure base object, so
runtime polymorphism no longer works. To avoid object
slicing, use base-class pointers or references instead
of passing or storing objects by value.

=========================================================
INTERVIEW ANSWER (30 SECONDS)
=========================================================

Object slicing occurs when a derived object is copied
into a base object by value. Only the base-class
subobject is copied, while the derived-specific state
is discarded. As a result, the copied object behaves
like a base object and runtime polymorphism is lost.
To avoid object slicing, always pass or store
polymorphic objects using base-class pointers or
references instead of by value.

